{"about":"67873974ceb0d9a148ec85758e48c5b8d855c5a4bbbf7f8b0fd2897ec557f92e","body":"The conjecture falsely equates 'preference for action' with 'belief in future reliability.' Popper explicitly distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven) and the pragmatic decision to act upon it. One can prefer the better-corroborated theory as the most rational basis for action without asserting that it is probably true or that nature is uniform. The preference is a methodological rule for critical testing, not an inductive inference about the future.\n[would settle: A definitive textual analysis of Popper's distinction between 'corroboration' (a report on past tests) and 'probability' (a prediction of future success), specifically examining his arguments in 'The Logic of Scientific Discovery' regarding the rationality of acting on unfalsified theories.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"TARGET CONJECTURE 67873974ceb0d9a148ec85758e48c5b8d855c5a4bbbf7f8b0fd2897ec557f92e"}